Short answer — use local try/catch in every async function, propagate errors to a single place, log + notify, and have global fallbacks for unhandled rejections. Below are concrete steps (from the conversation) and a compact code example you can drop into an app.

Key practices (from the excerpts)
- Wrap awaited code in try/catch inside async functions and either handle the error or rethrow / pass to next(err). (see socket and MessageService examples)
- For Express routes, call next(err) for centralized handling; place the centralized error middleware after routes. Log stack traces and return generic messages in production. (see centralized error middleware)
- Use a structured logger (Winston shown) and optionally an error-monitoring service (Sentry) and a notification path (axios). (see Winston + axios examples)
- Distinguish DB connection errors (e.g., mongoose.Error) and return 503 when appropriate. (see mongoose-specific 503 middleware)
- Add global handlers: process.on('unhandledRejection') and process.on('uncaughtException') to log/notify and consider graceful shutdown. (see global handlers)
- For socket handlers, use try/catch and emit an error event (e.g., errorMessage) to the client instead of letting the promise reject unobserved. (see socket examples)
- Clean up / finalize work in finally blocks where needed, and avoid sending multiple responses (return after res.send). (see "Cannot set headers" guidance)
- Consider retry or backoff for transient errors (DB/Redis/network) rather than immediate failures. (see retry/backoff suggestions)

Code example (combines Winston logging, Express async route + centralized middleware, socket handler, global handlers, and a notification function)
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const { createLogger, format, transports } = require('winston');
const axios = require('axios');
const Sentry = require('@sentry/node'); // optional

// Init Sentry (optional)
Sentry.init({ dsn: process.env.SENTRY_DSN, tracesSampleRate: 0 });

// Winston logger (from excerpts)
const logger = createLogger({
  level: 'info',
  format: format.combine(format.timestamp(), format.simple()),
  transports: [new transports.Console(), new transports.File({ filename: 'error.log' })],
});

// Simple notification helper (excerpt uses axios)
async function sendNotification(error) {
  try {
    await axios.post(process.env.ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL, { text: `${error.message}\n${error.stack}` });
  } catch (notificationErr) {
    logger.error('Failed to send notification:', notificationErr);
  }
}

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// Example async route handler — always try/catch and forward errors
app.post('/api/do-work', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = await someAsyncOperation(req.body); // await protected by try/catch
    res.status(200).json(result);
  } catch (err) {
    // add context, then forward to centralized middleware
    logger.error('Error in /api/do-work:', err);
    next(err);
  }
});

// Centralized error-handling middleware (place after routes) — logs, Sentry, differentiates mongoose errors (excerpt)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log stack trace
  logger.error(err.stack || err);
  // Capture in Sentry if configured
  Sentry.captureException(err);

  // Example: mongoose errors -> 503
  if (err && err.name && err.name.startsWith('Mongo')) {
    res.status(503).send('Service Unavailable');
  } else {
    // In production, send generic message to avoid leaking internals
    res.status(500).json({ error: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message });
  }
});

// Graceful global handlers (from the excerpts)
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
  logger.error('Unhandled Rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);
  Sentry.captureException(reason);
  sendNotification(reason).catch(() => {});
  // Consider graceful shutdown for critical apps
});
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  logger.error('Uncaught Exception:', err.stack || err);
  Sentry.captureException(err);
  sendNotification(err).catch(() => {});
  // For uncaughtException it's often safest to exit after cleanup:
  process.exit(1);
});

// Socket example: use async/await inside handlers and catch errors (excerpt)
const server = http.createServer(app);
const io = require('socket.io')(server);
io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  socket.on('chatMessage', async (message) => {
    try {
      const processed = await processMessage(message);
      // emit to room (use socket.to/ io.emit as needed)
      socket.to(socket.room).emit('chatMessage', processed);
    } catch (err) {
      logger.error('Error processing chatMessage:', err);
      // notify client gracefully
      socket.emit('errorMessage', { error: 'Failed to process message' });
      // optionally notify ops
      sendNotification(err).catch(() => {});
    }
  });
});

server.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000);

Notes / small checklist (from the excerpts)
- Wrap every await in try/catch (local handling) and either recover or pass errors up.
- Use centralized error middleware in Express and make sure it's registered last.
- Log full stack traces with Winston (or similar) and capture to Sentry for crash reports.
- For DB-related errors, return 503 for service unavailability when appropriate.
- Add process.on('unhandledRejection') and process.on('uncaughtException') to log/notify and decide on shutdown strategy.
- In socket handlers emit an error event to the client rather than leaving promises unhandled.
- Use finally blocks to close/cleanup resources where necessary.
- For transient network/DB failures use retry with exponential backoff instead of immediate failure.

